You are estimating iteration counts for a TypeScript + vitest project with two codelines:

- **mocka** (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a`): transit fare logic. Source in `src/fares.ts`, tests in `test/fares.test.ts`. The defect (MOCK3-1) is a boundary condition in `fareFor`: the senior concession check uses `rider.age > 65` instead of `rider.age >= 65`, so riders aged exactly 65 are charged the adult fare instead of the concession fare. The fix is a single operator change in one function. Tests run via `vitest run`; type-checking via `tsc --noEmit`. The existing test suite covers adults, children, pass holders, and seniors past the boundary but does not yet assert the exact-boundary case (age 65).

- **mockb** (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b`): departure board rendering. Source in `src/schedule.ts`, tests in `test/schedule.test.ts`. The defect (MOCK3-2) is an off-by-one in `formatStops`: the loop bound `i < stops.length - 1` skips the final stop. The fix is a single bound change in one function. Tests run via `vitest run`; type-checking via `tsc --noEmit`. The existing test suite checks arrow separation and first-stop presence but does not yet assert that the last stop appears in the output.

Both codelines use ESM (`"type": "module"`), strict TypeScript (`"strict": true`, target ES2022, moduleResolution "bundler"), and vitest 2.x. Each codeline has exactly one source file and one test file. Both fixes are single-line changes to a single function, but each will likely require adding a failing test case to the existing suite before or alongside the fix.

